WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.685
What an eval is, precisely.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:08.941
Anthropic’s engineering post “Demystifying evals for AI agents” gives a vocabulary worth adopting.

3
00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:13.659
A task is one test case: defined inputs plus success criteria.

4
00:00:13.939 --> 00:00:18.888
A trial is one attempt at a task; you often run several, because outputs vary.

5
00:00:19.168 --> 00:00:24.352
A grader is the logic that scores some aspect of an attempt, and one task can have several.

6
00:00:24.632 --> 00:00:35.789
The transcript is the full record of a trial (outputs, tool calls, reasoning), and the outcome is the end state you actually care about, such as whether a refund was really issued.

7
00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:38.395
Before you can grade, you need criteria.

8
00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:48.979
Anthropic’s eval docs say good success criteria are specific, measurable, achievable, and relevant, and that most real applications need several at once.

9
00:00:49.259 --> 00:00:52.416
“The bot should be helpful” fails every one of those tests.

10
00:00:52.696 --> 00:01:00.205
“On our held-out set, the bot hands off every request on the escalation list, and invents no product features” passes them.

11
00:01:00.485 --> 00:01:17.275
The docs also give three design principles you’ll see throughout this studio: make evals task-specific (mirror your real distribution, including edge cases), automate grading where you can, and prefer more cases with a decent automated grader over a few hand-graded ones.

12
00:01:17.555 --> 00:01:20.947
Graders come in three kinds, which week 2 covers in depth.

13
00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:29.931
Code-based graders (exact match, a regex, a JSON check, “is this quote in the source”) are fast and reliable but can’t judge nuance.

14
00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:36.653
Model-based graders (an LLM judge) handle nuance but must themselves be checked against people.

15
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:39.899
Human graders are the most flexible and the slowest.

16
00:01:40.179 --> 00:01:45.000
A good eval uses the cheapest grader that is trustworthy for each criterion.
